    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1010">

                <pb facs="0932" n="922" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg922"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.5216/104: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-14T20:00:00-05:00">April 14, 1941—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>229. Your despatch 5421, March 5. Department feels that you should say to the
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs, either orally or in writing, that his reply to you
                    of February 26 is unsatisfactory; that while admitting that the case cannot be
                    proved, you are convinced that your mail has been tampered with; and that you
                    expect the Japanese postal authorities to take due measures to see that
                    repetition does not occur. You should state that you are making these
                    representations upon instruction from your Government.<anchor n="34"
                        xml:id="d1010-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1010-f1" n="34" xml:id="d1010-f1-content">
                <p>The Ambassador made oral representations to the Japanese Vice Minister for
                    Foreign Affairs on May 7 and left with him a personal letter on the subject
                    addressed to Mr. Matsuoka (125.5216/105).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
